[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: [1mhow notifications actually reach people.[0m This is where notification systems usually fail — not technically, but
  by becoming noise everyone mutes.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether anything leaves the app. An in-app feed (bell icon, unread count) is needed regardless — it's the
  durable record, and everything else is a copy of it. The question is whether email ships in v1. Email is the only
  thing that reaches someone who isn't looking at the app, which is most of the point of "notify me." But it drags in
  real infrastructure: SMTP credentials, a send queue with retries (you cannot send mail inline on a web request and
  stay responsive), bounce handling, and an unsubscribe path. That's roughly as much work as the rest of the
  notification system combined.

  The middle option — in-app for everything, email for a narrow high-signal subset like "assigned to you" — is what I'd
  actually ship, because it gets the reach where it matters without training people to filter you out.

  What's cheap to change later: which events send email, and adding a digest. What's not: the in-app feed's storage
  model, since email sends read from it.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m build the in-app feed as the foundation, and include email for a small, high-signal event set in
  v1. Defer digests and any per-user preference matrix until people complain about specific noise.
